# The three-valued X < Y of intervals with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One enclosure
# interface", "Comparisons"): yes when every member of X is below every member of Y, x_u < y_l; no
# when none is, x_l >= y_u; both false (unknown) otherwise and when an operand is empty. By the
# comparisons of T. Exact: unknown exactly when neither holds. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_less_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
input:         stored yl
input:         stored yu
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      interval xl xu
requires:      interval yl yu
ensures:       trit yes no lt x y x=xl,xu y=yl,yu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the bounds compared, and a test of emptiness (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeIntervalPredicates.v interval_less_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical operand

nx  = lt(xl, const inf)
ny  = lt(yl, const inf)
ne  = and(nx, ny)
a   = lt(xu, yl)
c   = le(yu, xl)
yes = and(a, ne)
no  = and(c, ne)
